learnings[1]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-07-31T06:28:28.432Z","harness:claude-code","A detector and its test cannot share an input. The harness-detection specs set one harness signal and inherit the rest of the environment, so they verify that detection responds to the ambient environment — the exact variable they are trying to control. The failure is invisible on CI because runners have no harness variables, which means the strictest-looking gate is running in the weakest environment available. When a feature reads the environment, its tests must construct the whole environment, and the clear-list must be derived from the same registry the feature reads so a new entry cannot reintroduce the hazard."
files[4]{path,scope,note}:
  src/core/shared/author.ts,project,detector registry declaring claude-code ahead of codex; the clear-list must be derived from this registry
  tests/unit/commands/claim-command.spec.ts,project,same hazard in the claim ownership detection test
  tests/unit/core/shared/shared-helpers.spec.ts,project,sets CODEX_THREAD_ID without clearing ambient claude-code signals
  tests/unit/sdk/agent-runtime-primitives.spec.ts,project,two ambient-signal tests in the SDK agent runtime primitives suite
tests[1]{command,scope,timeout_seconds}:
  node scripts/run-tests.mjs test -- tests/unit/core/shared/shared-helpers.spec.ts tests/unit/commands/claim-command.spec.ts tests/unit/sdk/agent-runtime-primitives.spec.ts,project,600
body: "## Reproduced 2026-07-31 at HEAD, zero source changes\n\n`node scripts/run-tests.mjs test` on a clean tree: **4 failed, 6,579 passed**.\n\n```\ntests/unit/core/shared/shared-helpers.spec.ts\n  x detects the harness after empty configured input while explicit blank stays unknown\n    Expected: \"harness:codex\"   Received: \"harness:claude-code\"\ntests/unit/commands/claim-command.spec.ts\n  x uses detected harness ownership when PM_AUTHOR and configured author are absent\n    Expected: \"harness:codex\"   Received: \"harness:claude-code\"\ntests/unit/sdk/agent-runtime-primitives.spec.ts\n  x uses ambient signals by default and records extensible effort and role provenance\n  x attributes update and close mutations to the detected harness\n```\n\nSame three spec files, same commit, with the ambient Claude Code signal removed:\n\n```\nenv -u CLAUDECODE -u CLAUDE_CODE -u CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID -u CLAUDE_CODE_ENTRYPOINT \\\n  node scripts/run-tests.mjs test -- <the three spec files>\n=> Test Files 3 passed (3), Tests 65 passed (65)\n```\n\n## Mechanism\n\n`src/core/shared/author.ts` declares harness detectors in priority order; claude-code (`CLAUDE_CODE`, `CLAUDECODE`) is declared ahead of codex (`CODEX_HOME`, `CODEX_CI`, `CODEX_THREAD_ID`).\n\nThe tests set the signal for the harness they want:\n\n```ts\nprocess.env.CODEX_THREAD_ID = \"test-thread\";\nexpect(resolveAuthor(undefined, \"   \")).toBe(\"harness:codex\");\n```\n\nThey do not neutralise the signals of every other harness. Under any harness declared **above** codex the ambient variable wins and the assertion fails. Under any harness declared below it, or under no harness at all, it passes.\n\n## Why this matters more than four red tests\n\n**The verdict on a commit depends on which agent ran the tests.** This repository is developed by a fleet of agents across several harnesses, and the local gate is the first thing each of them runs. An agent operating under Claude Code sees four failures on a pristine checkout and must decide whether it broke something; an agent under a lower-priority harness sees green on the identical tree.\n\n**The hosted pipeline is structurally blind to this class.** GitHub Actions runners carry no harness environment variables, so codex is the only signal present and every one of these tests passes in CI. The failure exists only where humans and agents actually work, and the gate that is supposed to be the stricter one cannot observe it. This is the inverse of the usual local-green/CI-red asymmetry and it is worse, because the passing signal is the one that gets published.\n\n**It falsifies a claim the tests are making.** These specs exist to prove that harness auto-detection is correct. They currently prove that detection responds to the ambient environment, which is what they are trying to control for. A test for environment-derived behaviour that inherits the environment is not testing the behaviour.\n\n## Fix shape\n\nHarness-detection specs must construct the full environment they assert against, not mutate one variable of an inherited one. Concretely: a helper that clears every key named in the detector registry, then sets exactly the keys under test, restoring afterwards. Deriving the clear-list from the registry rather than hardcoding it means a newly registered harness cannot silently reintroduce the hazard.\n\nThe general rule is stronger and belongs in the gate: no spec may read an environment variable it did not set. That is checkable — run the suite twice under two synthetic harness environments and require byte-identical results.\n\n## Relationship to adjacent owners\n\nNot the flake ratchet: this is fully deterministic given the environment, so a re-run never turns it green and quarantine is the wrong remedy. Not the harness provenance defect, which is about model and session fields being inexpressible rather than about detection precedence. It is a concrete instance of the local-versus-hosted parity gap, and it is a concrete instance of the self-verification limit: the harness identity detector is verified by tests running under a harness, so the detector and its oracle share an input."
